-- title:
--   Atkin–Lehner quotient at r induces a Pi-translate of rigidifications
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are primes with $\bar r \neq r$, and $N$ is a nonzero natural number with $r \nmid N$.
--
--   *Coefficient rings.* $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring, $\pi \in \mathcal O$ is irreducible, $\mathcal O$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements, and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (so $\mathcal O$ is absolutely unramified with residue field of cardinality $r$); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field which is the fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Next, $Onr$ is a characteristic-zero domain and an $\mathcal O$-algebra carrying an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, subject to: $Onr$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`); that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`); every element of $Onr$ satisfies, modulo that ideal, a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ (`hOnr_alg`); every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo that ideal (`hOnr_closed`); and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r}$ modulo that ideal for all $x$ (`hFr`). Thus $Onr$ plays the role of the integers of the completed maximal unramified extension, with $Fr$ lifting the absolute Frobenius, and $k := Onr/(\pi)$ its residue field.
--
--   *Quaternionic data.* $a, b \in \mathbb Q$ satisfy `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ ramifies $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a, b]$ (that is, every nonzero element of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a unit) precisely when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$. $\Lambda$ is a $\mathbb Z$-submodule of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ which is a maximal order (`hΛ`: it is an order, and any order containing it equals it) and contains every rational integer (`hΛℤ`). A map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathbb{Z}_{p^2}(r) \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^2}(r)$, with values in pairs of Witt vectors over the field of $r^2$ elements, satisfies `IsOrderCoord Λ r coord`: it is additive, sends $1$ to $(1,0)$, satisfies the twisted multiplication rule $\mathrm{coord}(mm') = (\alpha\alpha' + r\,\beta\,\sigma(\beta'),\ \alpha\beta' + \beta\,\sigma(\alpha'))$ for $\mathrm{coord}(m) = (\alpha,\beta)$, $\mathrm{coord}(m') = (\alpha',\beta')$ and $\sigma$ the Witt-vector Frobenius, is injective, has dense image modulo every power of $r$, and matches reduced traces with first coordinates.
--
--   *Base point and its formal module.* $A_0$ is a fake elliptic curve over $k$ for the data $(\Lambda, N)$, $X_0$ a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module of dimension two over $k$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`), $\theta_0$ a system of two-variable formal coordinates for the structural morphism $A_0.f$, and `hA₀` asserts `A₀.IsFormalModuleVia coord X₀ θ₀`: $\theta_0$ is a system of formal coordinates for the group law of $A_0$ with formal group $X_0.F$, and on nilpotent parameters the action of each $m \in \Lambda$ on points is computed by the series $\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}(m)_1) \oplus \mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}(m)_2)\circ\varpi$ of $X_0$.
--
--   *Drinfeld datum.* $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{p^2}(r) \to Onr$ is a ring homomorphism; $\Phi$ is a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $Onr/(r)$ which is special for the reduction of $\iota$ (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`); $M$ is a moduli package over $Onr$ at $r$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hM`); and $\eta$ assigns, to every commutative ring $B$ with a ring homomorphism $\psi : Onr \to B$ and nilpotent $r$ in $B$, a map from rigidified objects $\mathrm{Rigidified}\ r\ \Phi\ B$ to $M$-points. The hypothesis `hη` has three clauses: over Noetherian $B$, two admissible rigidified objects have the same image under $\eta$ exactly when they are isomorphic in the sense of `Rigidified.IsIsomorphic`; $\eta$ commutes with base change along ring homomorphisms compatible with the legs, applied to admissible objects; and every $M$-point over a Noetherian $B$ becomes, after passing to the members of a finite family of localisations $B_{f_i}$ with $(f_1,\dots,f_n) = B$, the $\eta$-image of an admissible rigidified object. Finally $E_0$ is a ring homomorphism from the centraliser of $\{\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}\,a\}_a \cup \{\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}\}$ in the endomorphisms of the formal group of $\Phi$ to $2 \times 2$ matrices over $K_0$, and `hE₀` states that $E_0$ is injective and that for some $m$ the image contains $r^{m}\mathrm M_2(\mathcal O)$ and is contained in $r^{-m}\mathrm M_2(\mathcal O)$.
--
--   *Comparison of reductions.* $\kappa : Onr/(\pi) \to Onr/(r)$ is a ring homomorphism compatible with the two quotient maps (`hκ`), and $\beta_0$ is a series over $Onr/(r)$ which is an isogeny $\Phi \to X_0 \otimes_\kappa (Onr/(r))$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`).
--
--   *The point map $\Xi$ and its properties.* $\Xi$ assigns, to every Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : Onr \to B$ such that the image of $\pi$ in $B$ is nilpotent, a map from rigidified curves $\mathrm{RigidifiedCurve}\ r\ \pi\ A_0\ B\ \psi$ (pairs consisting of a fake elliptic curve over $B$ and a rigidification of it with leg $\psi$) to $\mathcal O$-points $\mathrm{GPoint}\ \mathcal O\ M\ B$. Four hypotheses constrain it: `hΞleg`, that over $B$ with only trivial idempotents the leg of $\Xi(x)$ is $\psi \circ Fr^{k}$ for some $k \in \mathbb Z$; `hΞnat`, that $\Xi$ is compatible with base change along an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\varphi : B \to B'$ whenever the underlying curves and rigidifications are related by pullback along $\varphi$; `hΞiso`, that $\Xi$ takes equal values on two rigidified curves whose underlying curves are isomorphic compatibly with group law, $\Lambda$-action and level (`IsoVia`) and whose rigidification data are matched by morphisms $ib$, $uA$ and exponents $i_1, j_1$ with $ib$ followed by $x'.\varphi$, $uA$ and $[r^{i_1}]$ equal to $x.\varphi$ followed by $[r^{j_1}]$; and `hΞdef`, the defining property: over a connected Noetherian $B$ in which $\pi$ and $r$ are nilpotent, for a rigidified curve $x$ whose underlying curve has formal module $X$ exhibited by coordinates $\theta$ through $\mathrm{coord}$, there are $j \le 1$ and $t : \mathrm{Rigidified}\ r\ \Phi\ B$ with $t.X = X$, with `IsRigTransport θ₀ κ β₀ x.2 θ j t`, with $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and the leg $\psi\circ Fr^{-j}$, and with $\Xi(x)$ the point given by that leg and $\eta(t)$.
--
--   *Frobenius twist of the base point.* $A_{0}^{(r)}$ (written `A₀r`) is a fake elliptic curve over $k$ together with $prA : A_{0}^{(r)}.A \to A_0.A$ exhibiting it as the pullback of $A_0$ along the map $k \to k$ induced by $Fr$ (`hprA`), and $F : A_0.A \to A_{0}^{(r)}.A$, $V : A_{0}^{(r)}.A \to A_0.A$ are morphisms over $k$. The hypothesis `hFV` has nine clauses: $F$ and $V$ are homomorphisms for the relative group laws; each commutes with the $\Lambda$-actions; each carries points factoring through the level structure to points factoring through the level structure; $V$ after $F$ is multiplication by $r$ on $A_0$ and $F$ after $V$ is multiplication by $r$ on $A_{0}^{(r)}$; and, for every commutative ring $C$ of characteristic $r$ and every $x : \mathrm{Spec}\,C \to A_0.A$, the composite $x$ followed by $F$ followed by $prA$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the $r$-power Frobenius of $C$ followed by $x$, which identifies $F$ followed by $prA$ as the relative Frobenius of $A_0$.
--
--   *Conclusion.* Under these hypotheses, for every Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$, every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : Onr \to B$ with nilpotent image of $\pi$, all fake elliptic curves $E, E'$ over $B$ for $(\Lambda, N)$ and all morphisms $q : E.A \to E'.A$ and $q' : E'.A \to E.A$ over $B$ such that `IsAtkinLehnerQuotientVia r E E' q hq q' hq'` holds — that is, $q$ and $q'$ are homomorphisms for the group laws, are $\Lambda$-equivariant, satisfy $q$ followed by $q'$ equal to the action of $r$ on $E$ and $q'$ followed by $q$ equal to the action of $r$ on $E'$ whenever $r \in \Lambda$, have the kernel property that the image of a point $P$ of $E$ under $q$ is the unit exactly when $P$ is killed by every $m \in \Lambda$ with $m m^{*} = rn$ for some $n \in \mathbb Z$, and $q$ preserves the level structure — and for every rigidification $\rho$ of $E$ with leg $\psi$ and every rigidification $\rho'$ of $E'$ with leg $\psi \circ Fr$, satisfying the relative-Frobenius correspondence hypothesis (there exist $qb : \rho.E_b.A \to \rho'.E_b.A$ with $qb$ followed by $\rho'.g_b$ equal to $\rho.g_b$ followed by $q$ and $qb$ over the base; $uA : \rho'.A_b.A \to A_{0}^{(r)}.A$ exhibiting $\rho'.A_b$ as the pullback of $A_{0}^{(r)}$ along $\mathrm{residueLeg}\ \pi\ \psi$ with $uA$ followed by $prA$ equal to $\rho'.g_A$; $Fb : \rho.A_b.A \to \rho'.A_b.A$ with $Fb$ followed by $uA$ equal to $\rho.g_A$ followed by $F$ and $Fb$ over the base; and exponents $i, j \in \mathbb N$ with $qb$ followed by $\rho'.\varphi$ and the action of $r^{i}$ equal to $\rho.\varphi$ followed by $Fb$ and the action of $r^{j}$), the following holds.
--
--   Let $L$ be a nontrivial Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra with only trivial idempotents, in which the images of $\pi$ and of $r$ are nilpotent, let $\varphi : B \to L$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra map, let $E_L$ with $g : E_L.A \to E.A$ be a pullback of $E$ along $\varphi$ and $\rho_L$ a rigidification of $E_L$ with leg $\varphi \circ \psi$ pulled back from $\rho$, let $E'_L$ with $g' : E'_L.A \to E'.A$ be a pullback of $E'$ along $\varphi$ and $\rho'_L$ a rigidification of $E'_L$ with leg $\varphi \circ \psi \circ Fr$ pulled back from $\rho'$, let $X$, $\theta$ exhibit $E_L$ as a formal module through $\mathrm{coord}$ and $X'$, $\theta'$ do the same for $E'_L$, and let $j \le 1$, $t : \mathrm{Rigidified}\ r\ \Phi\ L$ with $t.X = X$, `IsRigTransport θ₀ κ β₀ ρL θ j t` and $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and the leg $(\varphi\circ\psi)\circ Fr^{-j}$, and $j' \le 1$, $t' : \mathrm{Rigidified}\ r\ \Phi\ L$ with $t'.X = X'$, `IsRigTransport θ₀ κ β₀ ρL' θ' j' t'` and $t'$ admissible for $\iota$ and the leg $(\varphi\circ\psi\circ Fr)\circ Fr^{-j'}$. Then $j' = j$, and there exists $t'' : \mathrm{Rigidified}\ r\ \Phi\ L$ such that:
--
--   (i) $t''$ is the $\Pi$-translate of $t$ relative to the ring homomorphism $(\varphi\circ\psi)\circ Fr^{-j}$, i.e. `Rigidified.IsPiTranslate` holds: $t''.X$ and $t.X$ have the same formal group law and the same $\varpi$, the action of $a$ on $t''.X$ is the action of the Witt-vector Frobenius of $a$ on $t.X$ for every $a$, and for some $c \in \mathbb N$ the composite of the action of $r^{c + t.n}$ on $t.\overline X$ with $t''.\rho$ precomposed by $X_i \mapsto X_i^{r}$ equals the composite of the action of $r^{c + t''.n}$ on $t.\overline X$ with $t.\rho$ precomposed by $\Phi.\varpi$ transported along the residue map of that leg;
--
--   (ii) $t''$ is admissible for $\iota$ and the leg $(\varphi\circ\psi\circ Fr)\circ Fr^{-j'}$, that is, $t''.X$ is special for the corresponding structure map, has height $4$, and $t''.\rho$ is an isogeny of height $4\,t''.n$ from the reduction of $\Phi$ to $t''.\overline X$;
--
--   (iii) $t''$ is isomorphic to $t'$ as a rigidified object: there are series $u, v$ over $L$ and $m \in \mathbb N$ with $u$ an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism $t''.X \to t'.X$, $v$ one $t'.X \to t''.X$, $v \circ u$ and $u \circ v$ both the identity, and the composite of the action of $r^{m + t'.n}$ on $t'.\overline X$ with the reduction of $u$ followed by $t''.\rho$ equal to the composite of the action of $r^{m + t''.n}$ on $t'.\overline X$ with $t'.\rho$.
--
--   This is the formal-module heart of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison in the quaternionic setting: on the Drinfeld side, the Atkin–Lehner quotient at the ramified prime $r$ is matched, after transport of rigidifications, with translation by the uniformiser $\Pi$ of the maximal order, together with the statement that the two transports have the same exponent $j \le 1$. It is used to prove that the $\mathcal O$-point of the moduli package attached to the quotient curve is the $\Pi$-translate of the point attached to the curve itself.
